Classes with member functions and inheritance — class Foo : Bar { I64 x; U0 Bar() { ... } }. HolyC is C++-lite; classes are required for TempleOS-style code.

Pointers and arrays — I64 *p, &x, *p, p[i], pointer arithmetic. HolyC is a systems language; this is load-bearing.

switch/case/default/goto — HolyC uses these heavily.

Ternary cond ? a : b and logical &&/|| — basic expression completeness.

Bitwise operators & | ^ << >> and compound assignment += etc. — systems programming essentials.

String formatting HolyC-style — "Value: %d\n", x; as a statement (not a function call). This HolyC idiom is not supported.

Preprocessor or D's version/debug — conditional compilation. HolyC has #define/#ifdef; D uses version(X)/debug. Pick one and implement it.

Memory functions — MAlloc/Free/CAlloc/MSet as builtins. Without these you can't write real HolyC code.

extern/import linking — calling C ABI functions. You have the import token but no resolution.

cast(I64)x instead of (I64)x (you can support both)
ref/out parameters
pure, nothrow, @safe, @nogc function attributes (optional, cosmetic)
foreach (i, x; arr) with index
arr[1..3] slicing and arr[$-1]
string as a real type (not just a token alias)
is expression, assert(expr)
scope (exit) { ... } for cleanup
alias Name = Type;
unittest { ... } blocks (fits your --test flag)
version (DEBUG) { ... } and debug { ... } (D's preprocessor)
__FILE__, __LINE__, __MODULE__

Phase 1 — CLI & debugging flags (1–2 days)
Wire up the missing flags your front-end can already support:

--help — trivial, just print the flag list.
-tokens — call your lexer in a loop, print tokens. You already have TokenTypeToString.
-ast — write an ASTPrint(node, indent) recursive function. Half a day.
-o — accept an output name (relevant once you have a file-producing backend).
-D — defers to Phase 4 (preprocessor).
This is cheap, unblocks debugging, and matches the hcc CLI surface.

Phase 2 — Expression completeness (3–5 days)
Add the missing operators and expression forms to the lexer and parser:

Logical: &&, ||, ! (you have !)
Bitwise: &, |, ^, ~ (HolyC uses ~ for bitwise not; D uses it for concat, so the dialect must choose one)
Shifts: <<, >>
Compound assignment: +=, -=, *=, /=, %=, &=, |=, ^=, <<=, >>=, ~=
Postfix/prefix ++/-- (tokens exist, parser doesn't handle them yet)
Ternary: cond ? a : b
Cast: both (Type)expr (HolyC) and cast(Type)expr (D)
Comma operator: a, b (for HolyC-style "%d %d\n", x, y; statements)
This is mostly mechanical lexer additions and parser-precedence work. Your bytecode VM will need new ops (BC_AND, BC_OR, BC_BIT_NOT, BC_SHL, BC_SHR, BC_CAST, etc.).

Phase 3 — Statements & control flow (2–3 days)
switch/case/default — with break semantics. Add BC_SWITCH/BC_TABLE_SWITCH or lower to if-else chains in codegen.
break/continue — your bytecode already has BC_JUMP/BC_JUMP_IF_FALSE; add loop-context tracking in the compiler.
do...while — trivial parser addition.
Labeled loops — outer: for(...) and break outer; (D feature; the AST already has named nodes).
goto — HolyC has it. D technically has it but discourages it. Decide based on how "HolyC-identical" you want to be.
Phase 4 — Preprocessor or D's version system (3–4 days)
You need conditional compilation. Two options:

HolyC-style: #define, #ifdef, #ifndef, #else, #endif, #include. Implement as a token-stream filter after lexing.
D-style: version (X) { ... }, debug { ... }, static if (cond) { ... }. Implement in the parser/semantic layer.
Given you already have module/import tokens (D-style), go D-style — version/debug/static if are cleaner and fit your dialect better. This unblocks -D and --help's promise of conditional compilation.

Phase 5 — Structs, unions, enums (1 week)
This is where it gets real. HolyC code is struct/class-heavy.

struct — value type with fields. Memory layout, alignment, offset computation.
union — overlapping fields.
enum — named constants, optionally with base type.
Member access — obj.field. Add AST_MEMBER_ACCESS node, . operator in parser, BC_LOAD_FIELD/BC_STORE_FIELD with field offsets.
class with methods — reference type, new, delete, method dispatch. For the bytecode VM this is a big lift; for a C transpiler backend it's just structs + function pointers.
This is the phase where you'll want to start thinking hard about your type system and symbol table — you can't do member access without knowing struct layouts.

Phase 6 — Pointers & arrays (1 week)
Pointer types — I64*, U8*, pointer-to-struct.
Address-of & and dereference * — &x, *p.
Arrow operator -> — p->field (HolyC/C-style) vs D's (*p).field. Support both.
Pointer arithmetic — p + 1 where p is I64* should advance by sizeof(I64).
C-style arrays — I64 arr[10], decay to pointer, arr[i].
D-style arrays — I64[] arr, .length, .ptr, slicing arr[1..3], $ in indexing.
MAlloc/Free/CAlloc/MSet as builtins.
Phase 7 — Functions & modules (3–5 days)
import resolution — parse imported modules, make their symbols available.
module declaration — namespace the current file's symbols.
Default arguments — U0 Foo(I64 x = 5).
Variadic functions — Print(format, ...).
ref/out parameters (D feature) — pass by reference.
Function pointers — U0 (*fp)(I64), calling fp(x).
extern(C) / extern(Windows) — for C ABI interop (unblocks -clibs).
Phase 8 — String formatting (2–3 days)
HolyC's defining idiom:

c

"Value: %d, Name: %s\n", x, name;
This is a statement-level expression that calls Print under the hood. You need to:

Parse string-literal , expr-list ; as an implicit Print(string, expr-list) call.
Implement format-specifier parsing (%d, %s, %x, %c, %.3f, etc.).
Wire it to your VM's I/O.
Phase 9 — Native code backend (long-term)
Only after the language is complete in the VM. Pick one:

C transpiler (recommended): emit .c, shell out to gcc/tcc. Matches hcc's strategy but easier.
x86_64 asm emitter: match hcc directly, emit .s, use as/ld.
LLVM via llvm-d or C API: most work, best optimization.
